241	   * thumbnails. For example, you might want to load an image, start an animation if the
242	   * asynchronous image load succeeds and perform some fallback action if it fails. If you're using
243	   * a single primary request, {@link RequestListener} will work for this. However, if you then
244	   * decide to try to make things more performant by adding a thumbnail (or multiple thumbnails),
245	   * {@link RequestListener} is awkward because either you only add it to the main request and it's
246	   * not called when the thumbnails complete (which defeats the purpose) or it's called for every
247	   * request and it's hard to keep track of when the overall request has failed. A better option
248	   * than using {@link RequestListener} to track the state of the UI then is to use {@link Target}
249	   * instead. {@link Target#onResourceReady(Object, Transition)} will be called when any thumbnail
250	   * finishes, which you can use to trigger your animation starting. {@link
251	   * Target#onLoadFailed(Drawable)} will only be called if every request in the chain, including the
252	   * primary request, fails, which you can use to trigger your fallback behavior. Be sure to pick an
253	   * appropriate {@link Target} subclass when possible, like {@link
254	   * com.bumptech.glide.request.target.BitmapImageViewTarget} or {@link
255	   * com.bumptech.glide.request.target.DrawableImageViewTarget} when loading into {@link ImageView}
256	   * or {@link com.bumptech.glide.request.target.CustomTarget} when using custom rendering. Don't
257	   * forget to call {@code super()} in the {@code ImageViewTarget}s.
